faqs:
  - q: "How does this tool generate cryptographically strong passwords?"
    a: "The generator uses the native Web Cryptography API (crypto.getRandomValues) with rejection sampling to eliminate modulo bias. This draws high-entropy randomness directly from your operating system's cryptographic random pool."
  - q: "What is an EFF passphrase and why is it secure?"
    a: "A passphrase combines 4 to 6 random dictionary words (e.g. 'Acid-Acorn-Acre-Acts'). Because each word is drawn independently from a 1,296-word curated list, a 5-word passphrase provides over 51 bits of entropy while remaining easy for humans to remember and type."
  - q: "Are generated passwords saved in my browser or sent to a server?"
    a: "Never. For strict privacy and security, passwords exist purely in temporary browser memory. They are never stored in localStorage, never placed in URL query parameters, and never transmitted over the internet."
  - q: "What is the recommended password length for modern online security?"
    a: "NIST Special Publication 800-63B recommends a minimum of 16 characters for critical accounts. Passwords of 16 characters with mixed character sets provide over 90 bits of Shannon entropy, rendering brute-force attacks infeasible."
  - q: "Why should I exclude similar characters like 'O' and '0'?"
    a: "Excluding ambiguous characters (such as uppercase 'O' vs digit '0', or lowercase 'l' vs uppercase 'I') prevents transcription errors when typing passwords manually on mobile keyboards or reading paper recovery backups."
sources:
  - label: "NIST Special Publication 800-63B: Digital Identity Guidelines"
    url: "https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-3/sp800-63b.html"
  - label: "W3C Web Cryptography API Specification"
    url: "https://www.w3.org/TR/WebCryptoAPI/"
  - label: "Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF) Dice-Generated Passphrases"
    url: "https://www.eff.org/dice"
